# Feature Documentation (`docs/`)

When creating or updating a feature in `src/app`, `src/lib`, `src/components`, or `src/db`, keep `docs/` in sync in the same change.

## What to document

- Purpose of the feature and when a user encounters it
- Routes / entry points involved (`src/app/.../page.tsx`, server actions, API routes)
- Auth helpers and data access (`requireAdmin`, `auth.api.*`, Drizzle queries) — inputs, auth requirements, side effects
- Important security or ownership rules (who can call what)
- Env vars, migrations, or setup steps if any

## Where to put it

- **Update an existing doc** when the topic already fits (e.g. auth → `docs/authentication.md`, users → `docs/user-management.md`, architecture → `docs/architecture.md`).
- **Add a new `docs/<feature-name>.md`** when there is no clear home — use lowercase kebab-case filenames.
- Cross-link from related docs when helpful; do not duplicate long explanations across files.
- QA cases still go under `docs/qa/<feature>.test-cases.md` (see feature-testing rule).

## How to write it

- Keep docs actionable and short: what it does, how it works, how to use/test it.
- Prefer concrete paths and function names over vague descriptions.
- Do not invent behavior — document what the code actually does.

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD — vague, no pointers -->

## Profiles

Users can update profiles somehow.

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->

## Role change

- Route: `src/app/(dashboard)/users/[id]/page.tsx`
- Server action: `setUserRole` (`src/app/(dashboard)/users/actions.ts`) — calls `requireAdmin()` then `auth.api.setRole`
- Side effect: audit row via Better Auth hooks in `src/lib/auth.ts`
```

## Checklist

1. Did this change introduce or alter user-facing or API behavior?
2. Is there an existing `docs/*.md` to update, or do we need a new file?
3. Are routes, server actions, and auth/ownership called out with real paths/names?
4. Would another engineer know how to find and safely change this feature from the doc alone?
5. Did you also update `docs/qa/<feature>.test-cases.md` and run `pnpm verify` (typecheck, lint, test, build)?
